## Decision and scope

Proposed replacement for [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).
The accepted historical decision is preserved. The desk must adopt this replacement before
its procedure can be treated as changed. This review covers the supplied 4 October export,
not a live query of the desk's current queue.

[The incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls the date reports one known issue with one fix queued.
The executable cause table contradicts that claim. Separate the shared parser problem from
the booking form hint problem. A parser fix alone does not fix the form's misleading hint.

## Problems and counts

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

**Parser:** the shared parser reads month first although the tenant writes day first.
For example, Harbour Dental's report R-41200 says 7 April became 4 July; R-41201 corrects
the direction of its own report. Use the tenant's locale, not the reporter's browser locale.
Validate the parser with ambiguous dates in both directions and reject impossible dates.

**Hint:** booking-v3 displays MM/DD/YYYY while its form reads dates in the day-first tenant's
order. This affects form reports for Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour. Align the
visible hint with the form's parsing order and test submission through the actual form.
R-41230 and R-41241 illustrate this group. App and email reports from these same tenants
still belong to the parser group under the supplied classifier.

Counts above are computed from the data with uniqueById, then filtered to mergedInto 4471,
then grouped by causeOf using the tenant directory. Overlapping export pages must not inflate
counts. Intake tags are guesses; neither they nor subject text establish cause.

<Callout kind="warn">
The one-day-move notes identify reports whose wording does not establish a month/day swap.
They remain included in the parser total under the supplied classifier, not extra reports or
a proven third cause. Preserve their bodies and investigate actual timestamps, time zones and
reproduction steps before concluding what caused them. R-41620 also says the reporter stopped
reporting: the queue does not measure everyone affected.
</Callout>

## What the queue shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received time and merged time measure different events. Reports started arriving in April;
a later batch of merges is desk activity, not proof that the product failure started then.
Reports continue through the export date. The desk keeps working: refresh the export and
recompute before using these counts for a later queue review. This chart has a fixed export
window; extend its end date when reviewing newer reports.

## How to triage now

Look up the tenant's locale and form version, read the body and identify the channel.
Check the cause and merge only reports with the same supported cause. Keep unrelated
problems separate even when their subjects match. Missing tenant data or uncertain evidence
means investigate, not automatic merge. Matching classifier output is a triage rule, not
proof that every report has the same underlying defect.

<DedupeExplorer />

The examples demonstrate that identical subjects can describe different causes, and different
subjects can describe the same cause. The old triage page and decision were reviewed in 2024;
the runbook's stale warning is a reason to check their claims against these views. The newer
incident review date does not make its one-issue conclusion correct.

## Engineering outcome — 5 October

After the first course submission, the [challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows)
published a desk update: source ticket #4471 closed on 5 October.

- **Parser: fixed.** Engineering reports `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
  [Repository issue 1](https://github.com/dancarlson/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
  is closed as completed.
- **Hint: won’t fix.** The booking-v3 form was retired.
  [Repository issue 2](https://github.com/dancarlson/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)
  is closed as not planned. Retirement is not a fix to that form's hint.

These outcomes are reported by the course's engineering update, not independently tested
against production. The export counts remain historical. The original one-issue claim and
stale triage rule still require the corrections documented here.

## Follow-up and verification

Track one engineering issue per supported problem, with counts and report IDs in the repository's
drafts and generated analysis. Preserve a separate investigation note for anomalous wording.
Re-run the build when data changes, review the rendered cause table, dedupe examples and queue
chart, and verify this page's desk reference. Engineering outcomes are recorded above; production behavior has not been independently verified here.

